706: `posse start`:
707: 
708: 1. Validates an optional `--name`, then the Brief. An explicit Task Name is safe lowercase kebab-case, at most 24 characters, and cannot be a Task ID; it need not match the title. When omitted, Posse derives a short display name from the title or uses `task` when no safe ASCII name can be formed. Repeated names are allowed, but lookup by a repeated name or title returns `task_ambiguous`; `t<n>` is the stable selector. For Change Tasks, the title uses conventional-commit form `type[(scope)][!]: description` (for example, `feat(app): enable outcome-first runs`) and becomes the initial forge pull request title unchanged, without an id or slug prefix; a Lead may set a later title with `posse retitle` (section 14).
709: 2. Checks `max_workers` (`worker_limit`). A Worker slot is held by every `spawning`, `working`, `needs-decision`, `blocked` or `stalled` Task and by every Change Task in `done` or `merging`, because its Worker stays live and can return to `working` for CI fixes, merge-target syncs and Lead instructions. A slot is released only when the Task leaves those states (merged, torn down, failed or lost), so a follow-up never exceeds `max_workers` and queued refill never takes a slot a PR follow-up needs. The cost is that Change Tasks awaiting merge delay queued Tasks. Review Round Tasks skip this check and are never refused or queued for capacity, because the merges that release done Change Tasks depend on those rounds.
710: 3. Resolves the Profile.
711: 4. Chooses the next unused Task sequence, skipping existing Task history and `tasks/t<n>/` directories, and checks the Task-bound branch `posse/t<n>` against Task history, local refs and origin refs in the repository or selected workspace Members. A collision returns `branch_exists` before Task creation; a concurrent sequence change refuses admission and can be retried. Unrelated workspace Member failures or collisions do not block admission. It validates explicit `base:` and `target:` branches on origin without fetching, resolves config and selected-Member overrides, and records target names and either pinned commit or live branch bases on the Task's plan. Per repository, an explicit `base:` wins; without one, a selected target (Brief `target:`, `repositories.<member>.target_branch` or `defaults.target_branch`) other than the default branch is also the base, frozen at admission unless the Brief has a `landed` prerequisite, in which case Posse resolves the selected branch tip when the Task is admitted after that prerequisite lands. A base may be another published Task branch to form a stack. `posse start` prints `branches` with each repository's `base`, `base_commit` and `target`, and adds a warning for each base that differs from its target. The Task's `seq`, stored `branch` and `start` intent are committed together; except for explicit maintenance adoption, the stored branch must match its allocated `seq`. It acquires a Sandbox (section 12), checks out `posse/t<n>` with `git checkout -b` (never `-B`) from the pinned base commit, the live selected base, or the fresh default branch (Change Review: from the Change branch; Report Review: from the Project default branch). Repository Sandboxes open through `worktree.open` as linked child workspaces of the Lead; Workspace Project Sandboxes open as tabs in the Lead workspace. Posse reports pane `display_agent` using the resolved harness, then labels the pane with its Task identity ([ADR 0039](adr/0039-repository-workers-open-as-herdr-worktree-children.md)). The Lead's workspace comes from a fresh snapshot: the one holding the Lead's labeled pane, or the recorded one if it is still labeled `Lead:<project>`; otherwise `lead_missing`. It does not create another Git checkout. The Worker pane cwd remains the Sandbox path.
712: 5. Runs the kind's `prepare` step, if any (below).
713: 6. Labels the root pane `posse:<project>:t<seq>`, writes `brief.md` and `launch.md`, then starts the agent with `agent.start` (name `posse-<project>-t<seq>-<launch>`, cut to 32 chars and never derived from Identity, where `<launch>` counts launches of this Task so a relaunch never collides with a name Herdr has not released yet; args = kind `auto_approve_args` + `model_args`/`effort_args` filled from the Profile + Profile `args`, preceded for Claude by the attribution-off `--settings` unless the Profile sets `attribution = true`). The canonical pane label and all internal identifiers remain unchanged. `launches` and its Launch Identity Record (Task id, launch number, Profile name, configured model value and whether that value is determinable) are committed together before each `agent.start`; relaunches append records and never replace earlier identities. Herdr types each launch and each exported variable as one shell line. A line over the 4095-byte limit of a canonical-mode tty, as in a `/bin/sh` pane, would be dropped silently, so such a launch is written to `agent-launch.sh` in the Task directory (`lead-launch.sh` in the Project directory for a Lead restart) as the exports plus a one-shot shell function named after the agent's command; the pane sources that file and `agent.start` types the bare command. A command that cannot be a POSIX function name fails at once with `launch_line_too_long`. A pane whose shell is not POSIX (`fish`, `csh`, `tcsh`, `nu`, `elvish`, `xonsh`, `pwsh`) cannot source that file and fails at once with `launch_shell_unsupported`; Worker panes need a POSIX shell for launches over the limit.
714: 7. Codex and OpenCode receive `Read <launch.md path> and follow it.` as an opening argument in `agent.start`; these are the existing opening-argument paths. Claude and Pi first become interactively ready, then receive the instruction through a focused-safe typed prompt because Herdr keeps an opening-argument start pending for the first turn, which prevents steering. Focus does not delay launch. After Herdr observes the first turn working, the Task becomes `working`. Relaunch uses the same per-kind delivery paths with `relaunch.md`. If recovery of an interrupted Claude or Pi start cannot type the prompt because its pane is focused, the Task remains `spawning` for a later retry; Posse raises a `spawn_waiting` Notice if that recovery remains unresolved past the launch retry window. A failed spawn with a written Brief keeps its initialized Sandbox for `posse relaunch` on the same branch.
715: 8. Refreshes the Worker's pane metadata after launch and relaunch. `display_agent` is set to the resolved harness (`pi`, `codex` or `claude`) before `agent.start` and the detected harness afterward, never the unique internal agent name. Worker tab labels end with `-t<n>` (not repeated when the name already ends with it), so a reported Task ID maps to a tab; reconciliation restores tab labels and metadata after launch, relaunch, Teardown and recovery. Posse sets no `posse_row` token. `identity.worker.display_prefix` is deprecated and has no effect. The full Task title, display name and Sandbox path are submitted as the pane title, but Herdr truncates long titles and token values. Metadata tokens `posse_title`, `posse_name` and `posse_branch` preserve the title, display name and actual Task branch within Herdr's limits; `posse_sandbox` holds only the Sandbox basename to avoid a misleading mid-path truncation. The full Sandbox path remains in the pane cwd and Task record. This does not change pane cwd, the canonical pane label (`posse:<project>:t<seq>`), agent name or Task ID.
716: 9. Displays the Task Name in `posse`, `posse list` and `posse show`; Notice rows and wake bodies lead with the full Task title. Tasks without a recorded name display their title. `posse start` reports the Task-bound branch, and `posse show --full` preserves noncanonical historical branch values. Internal Task IDs remain available for commands and canonical pane labels.
